incident 0a5d8a28-39ac-4c5f-9ac1-1ef74c521457  state triaging  anchor 15:13:06
triage: 13 services, severity critical, start from checkout, 13 unmeasured edge(s) crossed

states: triaging -> planning -> investigating -> synthesizing -> proposing
trajectory: 640145e5-de29-4f23-b168-fc708628573a
triage judged: investigate (medium confidence, suspects unknown)
  Four services including payment and checkout alert simultaneously at critical severity, which is not dismissible and no open incident covers it. Simultaneity across the checkout path suggests a shared cause but the alerts alone don't distinguish deploy, config, or a common downstream dependency.
investigation: 2 round(s), 8 dispatch(es), 24 step(s)

=== PROPOSAL ===
  ABSTAINED - no permitted action fits the evidence
  if wrong    : This abstention is wrong if a flagd query (or a change-log query scoped to flagd rather than to payment) turns up a recorded prior configuration for payment naming a flag value inside 15:09–15:11, which would satisfy revert_config's two preconditions and make a config_revert on payment the right action. It is also wrong if payment's environment — not flagd's store — is where the failing-path toggle lives, in which case a revert would clear the rejections. Either finding should reopen this proposal immediately. Note that even a successful revert would be hard to confirm from metrics here: payment's error-ratio series has one defined sample and an empty baseline [tr_463410507be2], so confirmation would have to come from the cessation of the 'Invalid token. app.loyalty.level=gold' lines in payment's logs, which recur every 10–30s and so would be visible within about two minutes.
  rests on    : tr_1778ce8ce3e6, tr_d3518340b0ba, tr_5d6996bcab13, tr_407873775724
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

context: 33679 pushed / 7364 pulled (pull rate 0.1794), 2 section(s) dropped

=== VERDICT ===
  fault class : feature_flag
  fix class   : config_revert
  confidence  : medium
  evidence    : tr_0d3e77248cba, tr_5d6996bcab13, tr_407873775724, tr_1778ce8ce3e6, tr_463410507be2, tr_d3518340b0ba, tr_60cfa54aac6a
  root cause  : Payment is rejecting a subset of Charge calls with an application-level 'Invalid token' rejection tagged with a gold loyalty-level attribute, and checkout surfaces that rejection to the frontend and load-generator as 'failed to charge card'. The rejection is raised inside payment's own request-handling code, arrives within 1-4ms (a fast business-logic decline, not a hang or timeout), and the payment span carries a flagd flag evaluation on the charge path. No deploy, image rollout or configuration edit exists for either payment or checkout in the 24-hour change log - the only entries are platform-automation first-creation records for the workload and service objects. A failure mode that (a) begins at a definite time (~15:11), (b) is segmented by a customer attribute rather than affecting all traffic, (c) is produced by the service's own code while the service stays up and keeps accepting traffic, and (d) leaves the change log empty, is a runtime flag flip in the flag store enabling a failing payment code path for gold-tier requests.
  OPEN        : The flag store itself was never queried. No specialist read flagd's configuration or history, so the specific flag name, its value, who or what set it, and the exact flip time remain unconfirmed - the flag evaluation span and the attribute-segmented rejection are circumstantial, not direct.
  OPEN        : Whether the rejection genuinely affects only gold-tier requests, or whether the loyalty attribute simply happens to be present on all sampled failures. Payment's charge-path logs [tr_407873775724] carry no branch-decision or reason line, so the segmentation cannot be confirmed from payment's own logging.
  OPEN        : The earliest sampled trace [tr_0d3e77248cba] shows a connection-refused dial to payment on port 8080 with no payment child span at ~15:10:56. This is unexplained and may be unrelated cold-start churn (payment's workload was created at 15:09:59) or may indicate a second, earlier fault.
  OPEN        : The ~1042ms currency Convert outlier in that same trace is unexplained and was not investigated; no currency specialist was dispatched.
  OPEN        : The error-ratio metrics for both services have empty baseline windows and traffic gaps [tr_ab5bfd8c7f3b, tr_463410507be2], so neither blast-radius magnitude nor onset shape (step vs ramp) is established. The claim that 13 services are affected rests on triage, not on measured evidence here.
  OPEN        : Nothing was gathered on the other nine alerted/affected services, so whether their symptoms all reduce to the checkout-to-payment path is inferred from trace topology rather than verified.

